# Plain product language with the user

Margo is not a programmer and explicitly asked: no technical terms, now or ever.
Handle the technical side yourself; talk to him only about what the app does.

## When writing to the user

- Estonian, plain everyday words. Describe things as he sees them in the app:
  screens, buttons, sounds, presets, songs, loops, the phone app vs. the web version.
- Do not mention file names, code, functions, lint, tests, types, store, commits,
  branches, builds, migrations, frameworks, line counts, or error messages.
- Translate risk and value into user outcomes, e.g. "saved presets could get lost
  after an update" instead of "the persist migration is untested".
- When proposing work, say what gets better for him (fewer breakages, faster fixes,
  new behavior) and roughly how long it takes; skip how it is done.
- If a decision is genuinely his, frame the options as product choices.
- Commits, code comments and other technical artifacts stay in English and technical;
  this rule only governs what is said to Margo.

## Example

- Bad: "App.tsx on 4000 rida, store'i migratsioonil pole teste."
- Good: "Rakenduse põhiosa on üks väga suur tervik, nii et iga muudatus võib kogemata
  midagi muud lõhkuda. Samuti pole automaatset kontrolli, mis tagaks, et sinu salvestatud
  preset'id ja lood pärast uuendust alles jäävad."
